<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: West Africa's Golden Age</h1><p class="ws-lede">The empires that grew rich on the Saharan gold and salt trade, and their southern neighbours.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/west-africas-golden-age">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 6 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Benin Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Kanem-Bornu Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Hausa kingdoms</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Songhai Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Mali Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Oyo Empire</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Key terms</h2><p class="ws-how">The path uses these words. Explain each one in your own words.</p><ul class="ws-terms"><li><b>Caravan trade</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Ivory trade</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Salt trade</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Savannah</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Trans-Saharan trade</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;Caravans carried gold, slaves and ivory north across the Sahara and salt, horses and textiles south, and this trade underpinned the wealth of Ghana, Mali and Songhai.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 6, <b>Kanem-Bornu Empire</b>: &#8220;Ruled for close to eight centuries by the Sayfawa dynasty, this Lake Chad empire held the route to Tripoli and tied the central Sudan to the Muslim world.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 10, <b>Golden Stool</b>: &#8220;The sacred throne of the Asante, believed to hold the soul of the nation; the British governor's demand of 1900 that it be surrendered provoked a war.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;The empires that grew rich on the Saharan gold and salt trade, and their southern neighbours.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/west-africas-golden-age. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: West Africa's Golden Age</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Kanem-Bornu Empire <span>from 700 CE</span></li><li>Hausa kingdoms <span>from 1000 CE</span></li><li>Benin Empire <span>from 1180 CE</span></li><li>Mali Empire <span>from 1235 CE</span></li><li>Oyo Empire <span>from 1300 CE</span></li><li>Songhai Empire <span>from 1430 CE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Key terms</h2><dl class="ws-key-terms"><dt>Caravan trade</dt><dd>Caravan trade is long-distance overland commerce carried by groups of merchants with pack animals, typically camels, travelling together for safety.</dd><dt>Ivory trade</dt><dd>The ivory trade is the commerce in elephant tusks, prized for carving and luxury goods.</dd><dt>Salt trade</dt><dd>The salt trade refers to the long-distance exchange of salt, a vital commodity for human diet and food preservation.</dd><dt>Savannah</dt><dd>Savannah is a type of tropical grassland with scattered trees, found widely across Africa between the rainforest and the desert.</dd></dl></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
